  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nDense fog is the main story this morning thanks to calm winds and \nrelatively warm dew points, which has allowed temperatures to \nimmediately crash down to dew points resulting in significant \nradiation fog. Nighttime microphysics satellite imagery shows a bank \nof stratus stretching across portions of southern MN with the dense \nfog forming quickly as soon as the cloud cover retreats, and as of \naround 230am local time the fog has become so dense in spots that it \nis widespread within the satellite imagery as well. With the fog \nincredibly dense, it will also take awhile to burn off this morning \nas temperatures will be very slow to rise due to lack of solar \ninsolation as well as being past the autumn equinox meaning our sun \nangle is becoming weaker by the day. Once the fog burns off, we \nshould see temperatures rebound quickly with highs today in the \nupper 60s to low 70s which is just a touch above average for this \ntime of year. \n\nThe leading edge of potential rain showers arrives overnight tonight \ninto early Tuesday as a surface low begins to develop along the \nleading edge of an approaching trough, reaching eastern Minnesota by \nthe late morning to early afternoon and continuing to spread \neastwards later in the day. A strong Canadian low will spin up late \nTuesday into Wednesday and when combined with the boundary already \nover the area from the initial weaker surface low, could result in \nsome training rain showers and potential localized flooding. At this \npoint the most likely area for training rainfall would be \nsouth/southeast Minnesota into western Wisconsin, however over the \nlast 24 hours guidance has trended slightly northwards compared to \nthe last few days. As a result of this northward shift within \nprimarily the GFS/GEFS, the NBM QPF forecast has also increased with \nthe highest rainfall amounts over southern/southeast Minnesota and \nwestern WI on the order of 2 or more inches. While the exact numbers \nare still changing with each forecast package, the overall trend is \nthe thing to monitor for now. As far as convection is concerned, \nforecast soundings show a small sliver of instability which could \nresult in some rumbles of thunder, however the profile is very \nsaturated with very high PWATS indicative of a rain loaded \natmosphere as opposed to a convective one. We are also still \nrelatively warm which will result in a higher freezing level, and \nwith our lack of instability we may not see an individual updraft on \na mesoscale strong enough to produce much in the way of lightning. \nWe expect most of the area to see at least a half inch of rainfall \nwith the lowest amounts in western Minnesota before the boundary \nstalls into Wednesday over the south/eastern part of our area.\n\nBehind the departing system towards the late week and next weekend, \ntemperatures will cool down with highs falling back into the low to \nmid 60s with a lack of other significant rainfall on the horizon. \nEnsemble guidance shows relatively little in the way of \nprecipitation until at least Sunday of next weekend, and even then \nit is a minority of ensemble membership within the GEFS/EPS showing \nany precip at all, let alone measurable. This continues throughout \nthe rest of the period with no significant precipitation signals \nthroughout, rather we stay in the 60s for highs and upper 40s to low \n50s for lows without any significant weather systems through the \nrest of the period.",
